00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.400
and so we're just getting an arcade experience.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:22.960
And I think it's not that these games are particularly bad, which some of them are.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:26.560
It's that it's kind of getting old.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:31.880
Well, I don't even know if it's the arcade port part of it that bothers me.

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:36.300
I think what bothers me about all this, with Taito specifically,

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.920
is that I'm just not really digging any of their games.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:42.520
Like, look, Legend of Kage is visually interesting.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:43.740
For the first level.

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.780
Yeah, it has a fun thing about it here and there.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:49.220
But for the most part, it kind of falls flat for me.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:52.440
Elevator Action definitely wasn't a big one for me.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.700
Burger Time, which is kind of like a fan favorite, didn't really do anything special for me.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:00.460
And so it's like, what are they consistently doing wrong?

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.440
That like other arcade developers that bring like, because, you know, think about it.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:09.620
Capcom and Konami, both of those are bringing their arcade games to the NES and we're liking those.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:11.340
What's Taito doing wrong?

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:19.000
I think they're now that you say that, I think they're too faithful of a port.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:26.420
But because Capcom, like they, they change their games up a little bit when they come to the home console, right?

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:27.920
Like, or was it?

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:34.100
Well, yeah, I'm not gonna, I think like, you know, Konami did probably more when it came to changing them up.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.040
But I think like, what I'm kind of gonna say here, and this might be out of line,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:45.220
but I think that what it comes down to is that Taito is making the more traditional arcade style game.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:51.500
Whereas like, you know, Capcom and Konami are definitely thinking about the arcade first and then porting these games.

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:55.240
But they're, they're like that next generation of arcade games.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:17:01.640
I feel like the Taito games are very similar to like, that Pac-Man era of like, arcade games.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.440
You know, the early 80s.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:08.240
Whereas like a game like Ghost and Goblins is like, yeah, that could very well be a console experience.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.680
Cause it's a long game that goes on for a while.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:15.440
You know, Gradius is a game that like offers a lot of player choice.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.400
You know, like a lot of different options that we haven't seen before.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:25.080
I think like, it's the, it's the repetitiveness of the, of the, of the gameplay and the actions.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:28.720
And then also like the super simple nature of the loop.

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:31.120
Exactly. You know, I think that's, what's killing me about this.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:35.780
Cause it's like, I don't know, would I really be that excited if Pac-Man, which by the way, does not come to the NES.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.040
But would I really be that excited if it did get an NES port?

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.640
Would I be like, finally, Pac-Man.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:51.280
And I think the, kind of to springboard off of that, I think that the biggest thing that, that maybe upsets me about this being, about this port is that.

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:54.280
It doesn't feel like a long enough experience.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:18:00.300
It doesn't feel, you know, it feels like either you get hit and you die and it's one hit KO for the most part.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.060
Or you kind of like learn the levels.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:08.060
And like, by the time you understand the levels, it's like, you know how to, you know the objective.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:13.060
But that being said, I did have a good time with this game.

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:14.740
I mean, I, I, I did enjoy it.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:20.020
It was fun. It wasn't, wasn't anything, you know, it didn't amaze me, but, but I was enjoying it.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.920
Like, I think this did better than some of the other arcade ports.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:29.420
Um, but also, as I've mentioned on the show before, I'm not a big fan of like traditional arcade style games.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:37.100
Right. I will say that, um, a lot of the artwork that was done for the game, even outside of the actual game was really cool.

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:41.960
Uh, there was a lot of like cinematic approach, especially in the Japanese artwork that was done for it.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:54.480
I think, um, earlier this week on our Twitter page, we've been posting some of that art and I definitely recommend going back and checking that out because they kind of do even like, you know, like they make them look like movies almost.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.300
And then it turns out, I was like, no, it's just this arcade experience you can go get.

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.780
And it's like, I like it as an arcade game.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:00.880
It's a very simple loop.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:14.280
Kind of reminds me of, um, Donkey Kong in, in some ways where it's like, you know, it's just four levels in this, in Kage's case, five, five levels that like to Joe's point, if you're good at this game, you can get in a loop and you really be addicted to the, you know, to that feedback.

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:20.680
But I think if you're someone like me, who's kind of like, well, there's just a lot going on here and I'm like really just fascinated by the jump.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:23.000
I think like it only held my attention for so long.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:27.420
Yeah. Oh, well that's, I'm sorry, Taito.

